ion":"The input in every pair has the same three parts: a background (0), a thick rectangular **frame** of one colour (A), and a filled **inner rectangle** of a second colour (B) inside it. The inner rectangle is not a clean rectangle. A few single B cells stick out from its edge into the frame. I'll call these **bumps**. Pair 1 has bumps at (5,6) on top, (7,12) on the right and (10,8) on the bottom. Pair 3 has bumps at (4,11) on top and (8,5) on the left.\n\n**What each bump does.** Each bump marks a line across the grid. A bump on the top or bottom edge marks its column. A bump on the left or right edge marks its row. Along that whole row or column, the output swaps colours:\n- every B cell (inner rectangle and the bump itself) becomes A, so the frame colour cuts through the inner rectangle;\n- every background cell becomes B, so the line continues as B from the frame out to the grid border on both sides;\n- frame cells (A) stay A.\n\n**Checking this against the demonstrations:**\n- **Pair 1.** In column 6, rows 5–9 change 4→1, and rows 0–3 and 12–14 change 0→4. Column 8 behaves the same way. In row 7, columns 4–12 become 1, and columns 0–1 and 14–16 become 4. This matches the listed changes exactly.\n- **Pair 2.** The bumps (3,7), (6,10) and (13,4) mark column 7, row 6 and column 4. The changed cells are exactly 2→8 inside those lines and 0→2 outside the frame on them.\n- **Pair 3.** Column 11 gets 3 at rows 0–2 and row 15, and row 8 gets 3 outside the frame. Both lines become 2 inside.\n\n**How to find a bump.** A bump is a B cell with exactly one B neighbour (4-connected). Cells on the rectangle's edge have three such neighbours and corner cells have two, so only bumps are picked out.\n- If that one neighbour is above or below the bump, the bump sits on the top or bottom edge, and its column is the line.\n- If the neighbour is to the left or right, the bump's row is the line.\n\nColours are read from the input. The background is the most common colour. B is the colour of the bumps. A is the other non-background colour.\n\n**Test input.** Frame A = 1, inner B = 8. The bumps are (4,6) and (4,10) on top and (9,2) on the left. So columns 6 and 10, and row 9, become 1 inside the frame and 8 outside it. Each line is one stage of construction.","code":"def transform(grid):\n    g = np.array(grid)\n    H, W = g.shape\n    bg = background(g)\n    cols = [c for c in np.unique(g) if c != bg]\n    # identify inner colour B: the colour having cells with exactly one same-colour neighbour\n    bumps = []\n    B = None\n    for c in cols:\n        for y in range(H):\n            for x in range(W):\n                if g[y, x] != c:\n                    continue\n                nb = []\n                for dy, dx in ((-1, 0), (1, 0), (0, -1), (0, 1)):\n                    yy, xx = y + dy, x + dx\n                    if 0 <= yy < H and 0 <= xx < W and g[yy, xx] == c:\n                        nb.append((dy, dx))\n                if len(nb) == 1:\n                    bumps.append((y, x, nb[0], c))\n    # keep bumps of the colour that is enclosed (inner); pick the colour with most bumps\n    from collections import Counter\n    cnt = Counter(b[3] for b in bumps)\n    B = cnt.most_common(1)[0][0]\n    A = [c for c in cols if c != B][0]\n    out = g.copy()\n    for (y, x, (dy, dx), c) in bumps:\n        if c != B:\n            continue\n        if dx == 0:  # vertical neighbour -> column line\n            line = out[:, x]\n            src = g[:, x]\n            desc = f\"column {x}\"\n        else:\n            line = out[y, :]\n            src = g[y, :]\n            desc = f\"row {y}\"\n        line[src == B] = A\n        line[src == bg] = B\n        emit(out, f\"Bump at ({y},{x}) marks {desc}: inner colour {B} becomes frame colour {A}, background becomes {B} out to the border.\")\n    emit(out, \"All bump lines drawn.\")\n    return out\n","candidate_tactics":{"objects":[{"source":"final explanation","text":"**How to find a bump.** A bump is a B cell with exactly one B neighbour (4-connected)."}],"selection":[{"source":"final explanation","text":"The background is the most common colour."}],"recolour":[{"source":"final explanation","text":"Along that whole row or column, the output swaps colours:"}],"layers":[{"source":"final explanation","text":"Pair 1 has bumps at (5,6) on top, (7,12) on the right and (10,8) on the bottom."},{"source":"final explanation","text":"Pair 3 has bumps at (4,11) on top and (8,5) on the left."},{"source":"final explanation","text":"The bumps are (4,6) and (4,10) on top and (9,2) on the left."}]},"provenance":{"reference_assisted":false,"query_labels_used_for_rule_diagnosis":false},"artifact_sha256":"7c26b521142262e98a0bfbc05a8b0ab9dbadae3828a0c678cff81461c9ac2f93","task_sha256":"64897d48f71348ca23e102bf44067450e9117a3ee6ae14bd1cb47f0277dd2938","validation":"Frozen corpus recorded successful replay on teaching fixtures; synthetic generalization not established."}
